    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5p6drec",
      "content": "Chhattisgarh is drained by rivers belonging to three major river systems: Mahanadi system (draining northward and eastward into Bay of Bengal), Godavari system (southern CG), and Ganga system (small area in north via Son river). The Mahanadi basin covers the largest part of the state."
    },
    {
      "id": "xe56nbr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10.2 Important Facts About Mahanadi"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aqgtf14",
      "content": "The Mahanadi is the lifeline of Chhattisgarh. It originates from the Sihawa hills in Gariaband district and flows northeast through the state before entering Odisha, where it drains into the Bay of Bengal. The river is about 885 km long in total, with about 286 km in Chhattisgarh. The Gangrel Dam (Dr. Ravishankar Sagar) on the Mahanadi near Dhamtari is the largest reservoir in Chhattisgarh."
    },
    {
